Many applications in human pluripotent stem cell (PSC) research require the genetic modification of PSCs to express a transgene in a stable and dependable manner. Random transgene integration commonly results in unpredictable and heterogeneous expression. We describe a protocol for the derivation of clonal populations of human embryonic stem cells or induced pluripotent stem cells (iPSCs) expressing a transgene from a single copy of an integrated lentiviral vector that is mapped to the genome. Using optimized transduction conditions, followed by single-cell subcloning and a round of antibiotic selection, we find that approximately half of the colonies retrieved contain a single vector copy. After expansion, the majority of these are confirmed to be clonal. The vector/ genomic DNA junction is sequenced and the unique integration site is mapped to the genome. This protocol enables the efficient derivation of genetically modified PSCs containing an integrated transgene at a known genomic site in ${\sim}7$ weeks.
## INTRODUCTION
Human PSCs, including embryonic stem cells (ESCs)1 and $\mathrm{iPSC}s^{2}$ , can self-renew indefinitely in culture and can be directed to dif­ ferentiate toward diverse cell fates, thereby giving rise to cell types of all three embryonic germ layers (endoderm, mesoderm and ectoderm). Thus, PSCs offer an invaluable research tool to study development and to model human disease. Morever, there is the intriguing possibility of their future use as cell factories to generate cell products for use in regenerative medicine. Many applications of PSCs (discussed below) require their genetic modification.
Retroviral $\upgamma\cdot$ -retroviral or lentiviral) vectors present the most commonly used tools for the permanent genetic modification of human cells, owing to their highly efficient delivery and versatility3–6. However, a commonly encountered problem is heterogeneity in the expression of the vector-encoded transgene obtained in cell populations; this is due to position effects conferred by different integration sites. Furthermore, retroviral vectors are often subject to transcriptional silencing shortly after transduction or extinction (progressive silencing of an initially expressing vector), through known mechanisms of gene repression, such as DNA methyla­ tion and histone modification, as well as through uncharacterized mechanisms7. These phenomena are particularly prominent in $\mathrm{PSC}s^{3,8}$ . Moreover, the semirandom pattern of retroviral integra­ tion and the high propensity of integration near or inside active genes and promoter regions9,10 often result in dysregulation of endo­ genous gene expression. The latter may cause artifactual pheno types in vitro11, obscuring the interpretation of experimental data obtained with genetically modified cells; in addition, it may lead to clonal dominance or overt cellular transformation and oncogenesis in vivo12–14.
## Development of the protocol
To overcome the above-mentioned problems, we developed a pro­ tocol15 that allows the derivation of PSC clones harboring a single copy of a lentiviral vector integrated at a favorable genomic site, thereby permitting adequate expression levels without perturbing endogenous gene expression. To do this, we transduce PSCs with the vector under optimized conditions that favor single, rather than multiple, vector integrations in individual cells. We then select and expand several clones with randomly integrated single-vector cop­ ies, map the integration site to the human genome and characterize it with relation to genomic landmarks (such as coding genes, micro­ RNA genes and ultraconserved regions). Transgene expression, as well as expression of endogenous genes in the neighborhood of the integration site, can also be assessed. Thus, desirable clones can be selected on the basis of the integration site and the transgene expression levels.
In a recent study, we used a lentiviral vector, TNS9.3/fNG, to transfer a normal copy of the human gene encoding $\upbeta$ -globin— linked to regulatory elements of its genomic locus (Fig. 1)—into iPSCs derived from subjects with $\upbeta$ -thalassemia major15. We used the protocol described here to generate clonal iPSC lines harboring a single copy of the vector that we mapped to the human genome to select desirable integration sites that we refer to as ‘genomic safe harbors’15. Safe harbor sites of transgene integration are defined as sites that permit desired and predictable levels of transgene expres­ sion without perturbing endogenous gene expression by insertional mutagenesis.
## Applications and limitations of the protocol
This protocol should be readily adaptable to all applications that require precise and robust genetic modification of human PSCs and can be implemented by investigators with standard stem cell culture and molecular biology expertise. Genetic modification of human cells may be used for overexpression or knockdown through shRNA of a gene for functional studies16–18. Applications particularly ­relevant in stem cell research include genetic marking or generation of reporter PSC lines. The latter involves the transfer of a reporter transgene in which the expression is regulated in a ­tissue- or develop­mental stage–specific manner for tracking of cells and lineage analyses. These can permit optimization of ­protocols of directed differentiation of PSCs to specific fates—an area of intense interest in PSC research—and/or cell purification19–23. Ectopic expression or suppression of a key developmental gene can also be used to induce or enhance differentiation of PSCs toward specific lineages16,17.
The benefit of isolating clonal genetically engineered cells is that homogeneous transgene expression can be achieved (Fig. 2). Furthermore, clones with different levels of transgene expression can be generated (Fig. 2) and used to tease apart different outcomes of varied levels of overexpression or knockdown of a gene in PSCs and in their differentiated derivatives24.
This approach can also be useful for future clinical applications of PSCs to introduce a therapeutic, suicide or marker gene. Careful selection of integration sites can minimize risks of insertional
