­oncogenesis and overcome some safety concerns associated with the use of PSC derivatives in cell therapy, although all candidate sites will eventually need to be carefully validated25.
A potential limitation of this method is that the frequency of long-term transgene expression and silencing from different genomic sites, especially sites that are remote from endogenous genes, is presently unknown. Transgene silencing is likely to be influenced not only by the site, but also by the genetic elements carried on the vector. Therefore, the frequency of clones expressing the transgene at physiologically relevant levels may vary depending on the choice of vector and the experimental setting.
## Comparison with other methods
In this protocol, we describe transduction with a lentiviral vector, but $\gamma\mathrm{.}$ -retroviral or other types of integrating vectors can be used with little adaptation of the protocol. Methods for generating per­ manently genetically modified cells other than retroviral vectors include transfection of plasmid or bacterial artificial chromosome (BAC) DNA by electroporation or the related method of nucleo­ fection, followed by antibiotic selection26–30. The use of BACs to create transgenic reporter PSC lines has the advantage over other methods in that it allows the transfer of large genomic segments (up to $350\mathrm{kb}$ , in contrast to the limited packaging capacity of retroviral vectors), and thus may allow more faithful regulation of reporter gene expression. In addition, it does not require the precise char­ acterization of cis-regulatory elements that are required to drive reporter expression31. Disadvantages include the low efficiency of gene transfer and the relative difficulty of modification and produc­ tion of BACs. BACs and plasmids integrate randomly, and often in more than one tandem repeats (concatamers). Therefore, the expression in different derived lines is unpredictable and prone to position effects. In addition, integration is random, resulting in junctions of plasmid or BAC DNA and genomic DNA that are not defined, hampering their mapping to the genome and characteriza­ tion using standard molecular screening methods.
Another way to permanently genetically modify cells is by targeted integration (also referred to as the knock-in approach) through site-specific homologous recombination. Although this approach has proved to be quite challenging in human $\mathrm{PSC}s^{32}$ —in contrast to mouse ESCs, in which it has long been used for mouse transgenesis—few recent reports have used homologous recom­ bination-based strategies33–35. Although future improvements may render this approach more generally applicable, homo­logous recombination is at present hampered by very low efficiency, necessitating the screening of a large number of clones33. The effi­ ciency can be improved with the use of donor constructs with long isogenic homology arms specific for each line, but this makes this approach more cumbersome. The efficiency of homologous recombination can also be substantially increased by introducing double-stranded DNA breaks through the transient delivery and expression of site-specific endonucleases (such as zinc-finger or homing endonucleases)36–38. However, the design and validation of a nuclease specific to each locus of interest is cumbersome and their potential genotoxicity because of off-target DNA breaks is unclear at this point and hard to monitor. Increased efficiency of homologous recombination has also been reported with BACmediated39 and adeno-associated virus–mediated40,41 delivery of the donor DNA. Gene targeting may be preferred for the gen­ eration of transgenic reporter PSC lines, but may be complicated by issues pertaining to the accessibility of different genomic loci. With regard to applications requiring gene addition, the current unavailability of known safe harbor sites that can support adequate transgene expression in several lineages remains a limitation that may be overcome with careful validation and characterization of candidate sites15,33,38.
## Experimental design
Vector design. To facilitate the identification and selection of singlecopy clones, we recommend that the vector be designed to encode, in addition to the genetic material of interest, a selectable marker. The latter may be a fluorescent protein, a cell surface marker or an antibiotic selection gene that should be constitutively expressed in a polycistronic manner or driven by a separate promoter. If needed, the latter may be excisable, thus enabling its removal after the ­selection process is completed. The vector used in our study contains a ‘floxed’ selection cassette encoding two reporter genes simultaneously in a bicistronic transcript, the enhanced GFP (eGFP) and the neomycin phosphoryltransferase (Neo) gene, that confers resistance to neomycin driven by the constitutive human phosphoglycerate kinase promoter (Fig. 1). The advantage of using selectable markers is twofold: (i) it allows the easy identification of transduced cells, which comprise a small percentage of the total cell population, given that transduction needs to be performed at a low multiplicity of infection (MOI) for recovery of single-copy clones (see below for details); and (ii) it can facilitate the selection of cells that integrate the vector at sites that are permissive for transgene expression. If antibiotic selection is used, it is essential to carefully titrate the antibiotic in the given PSC line to determine the mini­ mum lethal dose. The use of feeder mouse embryonic fibroblasts (MEFs) that are resistant to the antibiotic is desirable for optimal selection, but not absolutely required. Antibiotic-sensitive MEFs can be replenished every few days during the application of selec­ tion. If a cell surface marker or a fluorescent protein is used, trans­ duced cells can be isolated by fluorescence-activated cell sorting (FACS) as early as $^{48\mathrm{h}}$ after transduction.
Optimization of transduction. The number of vector inte­ grations can vary greatly according to the vector MOI used for ­transduction42. Retrieving clones with a single vector copy requires transduction at low MOI. To ensure this, we typically transduce cells at titrated MOI (0.1, 0.3, 1), as shown in Figure 3 (see PROCEDURE Step 7). Two days after transduction, we ­harvest the cells as a single cell suspension and replate them to isolate single-cell clones (Steps 9–13). At this point, we carry out flow cytometry analysis (Step 12) and select the group with ${\leq}30{\mathrm{-}}40\%$ $\mathrm{eGFP^{+}}$ cells. For reproducibility, we perform trans­ duction in defined conditions, in cells seeded as single cells in feeder-free conditions (Fig. 4). To overcome the generally poor survival of human PSCs as single cells and low clonogenicity, we supplement the medium with the Rho-associated kinase inhibi­ tor Y-27632, which has been found to decrease apoptosis and increase subcloning efficiency43.
Ascertainment of single-vector integration and clonality. To measure the vector copy number (VCN), we perform multi­ plex quantitative PCR (qPCR) with a set of primers and probes specific to the vector and a set specific to the gene encoding human albumin (ALB) as an internal two-copy control (Steps 23–24). We use absolute quantification, which we consider to be more robust, although relative quantification can also be used. If iPSCs generated with permanently integrating vectors are used, the primers and probe should be designed to be specific for the gene modification vector. Otherwise, primers and probe recog­ nizing sequences common to all lentiviral vectors (such as gag) may be used. We use a standard curve consisting of tenfold dilu­ tions of a plasmid harboring both vector and albumin amplicons (Fig. 5). The standards correspond to a known starting amount of $10^{2}$ up to $10^{7}$ copies of the template. Taking into account, in the analysis, the fact that diploid human cells harbor two cop­ ies of the gene encoding albumin, the VCN is calculated with the formula: [Mean quantity (gag) / Mean quantity $(A L B)]\times2$ . DNA samples of single or known vector copy controls that are run simultaneously add robustness to the assay and are rec­ ommended, if available. To ascertain single-vector integration and clonality, we perform Southern blotting after digestion of genomic DNA with two different restriction enzymes (Fig. 6). The DNA probe is prepared by gel purification of a PCR or restriction enzyme digestion product, and is ultimately experi­ mentally tested for its specificity. In general, the length of the probe should be 200–1,000 bp and repetitive sequences should be avoided. The restriction enzyme and position of the probe are selected such that each integration site will generate a unique band: the enzyme must cut at least once inside the vector, but not anywhere between the position of the probe and the end of the vector (see example, Fig. 6, top). The size of the band is unknown and is determined by the most proximal restriction enzyme site in the genome. Therefore, enzymes that cut the human genome frequently should be selected.
Inverse PCR design. To map integration sites, we use inverse PCR (iPCR; Fig. 7). Any other method to retrieve and sequence ­vector DNA-genomic DNA junctions (such as linear amplificationmediated PCR, or ligation-mediated PCR) can be used, based on the investigator’s expertise. The design of an iPCR assay (Fig. 7) involves the selection of the following: (i) a set of two primers oriented toward opposite directions and located close to one end of the vector $5^{\prime}$ or $3^{\prime}$ ), e.g., in the long terminal repeat. If iPSCs generated with integrating vectors are used, the primers should be designed to be specific for the gene modification vector and discriminate between the latter and the reprogramming vector(s). (ii) A restriction enzyme (enzyme A) that cuts frequently in the genome (typically a 4-base cutter), but not between the position of the primers and the end of the vector; and (iii) optionally, a second enzyme (enzyme B, a rare 6-base cut­ ter) that cuts between the two primers and nowhere between there and the end of the vector. After digestion of genomic DNA with enzyme A, the DNA is ligated at very dilute conditions to favor intramolecular ligation. Thus, the DNA fragment containing the vector/DNA junction is circularized and can be amplified with the primers annealing in the known vector sequence and sequenced (Fig. 7). Sequencing reads of iPCR products are judged to be authentic integration sites if the sequences are adjacent to vector sequences at both ends (Fig. 8) and have a unique hit when aligned to the draft human genome (University of California Santa Cruz, UCSC Genome Browser; http://genome.ucsc.edu/). Genomic annotations can be obtained from UCSC Genome Browser for further characterization and evaluation of the integration sites. The integration sites retrieved should be confirmed by integra­ tion site-specific PCR, using one primer annealing to the vector end (which can be common for all integrations) and a primer annealing to the genomic DNA adjacent to the vector sequence (which should be customized for each integration site), followed by sequencing of the amplicon15.
Controls. The transduction of PSCs, especially when performed using a vector that was not tested previously, can be controlled by transducing in parallel a vector encoding eGFP or any other marker that can be easily monitored. To test for potential vector toxicity and for the specificity of detection of vector sequences in the molecular analyses, untransduced PSCs are essential controls during most steps of this protocol, including transduction, sub­ cloning and VCN determination by qPCR and Southern blotting. Untransduced cells should be processed in parallel with transduced clones during preparation of cell lysates and genomic DNA to exclude plasmid contamination.
