## B ox 1 | LENTIVIRAL VECTOR PRODUCTION AND TITRATION
## Lentiviral vector production by triple co-transfection of 293T producer cells
1. Day 1: Thaw a vial of 293T cells at $37^{\circ}\complement$ . Transfer the contents into a $15-\mathsf{m l}$ conical tube containing $10~\mathrm{ml}$ of 293T medium. Centrifuge the cells at $320g$ at $24^{\circ}\complement$ for 5 min. Discard the supernatant. Resuspend the cells in $10\mathrm{\ml\}293\mathrm{T}$ medium and transfer into a $100\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m m}$ dish. Incubate at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,\ 5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{_2}$ for $24{-}48\ h$ or until the cells reach $80{-}90\%$ confluency.   
2. Day 2: Coat one $100\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m m}$ dish by adding a sufficient volume of coating buffer to cover the entire surface of the dish. Aspirate off the coating buffer.   
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP 293T cells will likely detach when they become overconfluent after transfection unless seeded in poly-l lysine-coated dishes. 3. Aspirate off medium from 293T cells from Step 1, gently wash once with PBS and add $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ trypsin-EDTA. When cells visibly detach (after $2{\cdot}5\mathrm{\min}$ ), add $9\ \mathrm{ml}\ 293\mathrm{T}$ medium and thoroughly pipette the cells up and down against the bottom of the dish with a $10\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m l}$ pipette to generate a single-cell suspension.   
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP The 293T cells tend to form clumps and need to be thoroughly dissociated mechanically by pipetting for optimal replating. 4. Replate one-third of the cells in the coated $100\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m m}$ dish from Step 2 in a total volume of $10~\mathsf{m l}$ of 293T medium. To homogeneously disperse the cells, move the dishes forward and backward and side to side on a flat surface (do not swirl). Incubate at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,\ 5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ . $\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP the 293T cells should not be split too sparsely or allowed to become confluent at any time before transfection. Homogeneous distribution and optimal density of the 293T cells at the time of transfection is critical for the efficiency of transfec­ tion and, subsequently, the vector titer. Splitting more than 1:3 the day before transfection should be avoided, as it will result in less homogeneous growth across the entire dish.   
. Day 4: Inspect cells under an inverted microscope. When the cells are ${\sim}80\%$ confluent, gently replace the medium with $7~\mathsf{m l}$ of 293T medium prewarmed at $37^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ Incubate at $37~^{\circ}\mathsf C,~5^{\circ}/_{0}~\mathsf C0_{_2}$ for at least $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ .   
6. Prepare a plasmid/ $\mathsf{T a C l}_{2}$ mix by adding $10~\upmu\mathfrak{g}$ of the lentiviral vector plasmid, $7.5~\upmu\up g$ of pCMV∆R8.91, $2.5~\upmu\up g$ of pUCMDG, $50~\upmu\upiota$ of $2.5\mathrm{~M~CaCl}_{_2}$ and WFI to a total volume of $500~\upmu\upiota$ . Thaw $2\times1+135$ buffer at $24^{\circ}\complement$ .   
7. To transfect, transfer $0.5~\mathsf{m l}$ of plasmid/ $\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}$ mix into a $50\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m l}$ conical tube. While vortexing at low speed, add $0.5~\mathsf{m l}$ of the $2\times1185$ buffer dropwise using a P1000 pipette. Remove tube from vortex, pipette-mix up and down once, then add $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ of the new mix to the 100-mm dish of 293T using a P1000 pipette dropwise, scattering the drops uniformly to the entire surface of the dish. Mix by moving the dish forward and backward and side to side. Incubate at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,\ 5\%$ ${\sf C}0_{_2}$ for ${\sim}16\ \mathsf{h}$ .   
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP It is important to add the mix to the cells within 30–60 s after adding the $2\times1+135$ to the plasmid/ $\mathsf{T a C l}_{2}$ mix. A very fine sand-like precipitate should be visible under the microscope within an hour after transfection.   
8. Day 5: Aspirate the medium and replace gently with $10~\mathsf{m l}$ prewarmed 293T medium per plate. Incubate at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,\ 5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ for ${\sim}24\ h$ . 9. Day 6: Collect the vector-containing supernatant and discard dishes. Centrifuge at $1,000g$ at $4^{\circ}\complement$ for 5 min to pellet cell debris. Filter the supernatant through a $0.45\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter. Aliquot and store at $\mathtt{-80}^{\circ}\mathtt{C}$ .   
 PAUSE POINT Lentiviral supernatant can be stored at $-80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for several months. Vector titer will decrease with prolonged storage.
Avoid repeated freeze-thaw cycles.
## Lentiviral vector titration
1. Day 1: Plate approximately $1\times10^{5}$ MRC5 human fibroblasts per well of a six-well plate in $2~\mathsf{m l}$ of MRC5 medium and incubate at $37\ ^{\circ}\mathsf C,\ 5\%$ ${\mathsf{C}}0_{2}$ for $18\mathrm{-}20~\mathsf{h}$ .   
. Day 2: Aspirate off medium from one well, wash once with PBS and add $0.5~\mathsf{m l}$ trypsin-EDTA. When the cells detach, harvest, mix ell, and count using a hemocytometer. Record number for titer calculation (in Step 5).   
3. Thaw the lentiviral supernatant at room temperature. Transduce cells at fivefold serial dilutions of virus ranging from 500 to $4\upmu\upiota$ in a total volume of $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ of media with $4~{\upmu\up g}~{\mathsf{m l}}^{-1}$ of polybrene. Add $1~{\mathfrak{m}}!$ medium containing polybrene to one well as a mock control. Incubate at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $18-20~\mathsf{h}$ .   
4. Day 3: Aspirate off the medium containing the vector from each well and replace with $2-3~{\mathsf{m l}}$ of fresh medium.   
5. Day 4: Aspirate off medium, wash once with PBS and add $0.5~\mathsf{m l}$ trypsin-EDTA to each well. When the cells detach, harvest in FACS tubes, add PBS and centrifuge at $320g$ at $4^{\circ}\complement$ for 5 min.   
6. Analyze in a flow cytometer using the mock-transduced well as control, as described in Step 12 of the PROCEDURE. Estimate the percentage of transduced cells from each well. Use a well with a percentage of transduced cells between 1 and $30\%$ to calculate the vector titer using the formula: ( $\cdot\%$ transduced) $\times$ (total number of cells on day 2) $\times$ (dilution factor) / (total volume of transduction medium). For example, if transduction of 100,000 cells with $100~\upmu\upiota$ of lentiviral supernatant in $1,000~\upmu\upiota$ of volume (dilution factor 10) yields $10\%$ (0.1) transduced $(\mathsf{e G F P^{+}})$ cells, the titer is $0.1\times100,000\times10/1=1\times10^{5}\mathrm{TU}\mathrm{ml}\cdot$ $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1}$ (TU, transducing units).   
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP If the vector does not encode a fluorescent protein or surface marker to allow titer estimation by flow cytometry, the titer can be calculated by VCN determination using $q{\mathsf{P C R}}$ in genomic DNA extracted from the cells (as described in Steps 23–24 of the
PROCEDURE). In the latter case, it is essential to passage the cells for at least 10–14 d before extracting DNA to dilute out any plasmid contaminating the lentiviral supernatant.
