39| Day 19: Check the quality of digestion by visualizing the DNA under a UV transilluminator after staining with EtBr by immersing the gel in a tray containing a sufficent volume of water or $1\times$ TAE buffer with $0.5~{\upmu\up g}~{\up m\updownarrow}^{-1}$ EtBr and shaking gently for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Fig. 9). Place a fluorescent ruler alongside the gel and take a photograph to later calculate the size of any bands according to migration in relation to the DNA ladder. Cut away any empty areas of the gel to reduce its size and facilitate handling thereafter. Measure the gel dimensions. Carefully transfer the gel into a glass baking dish. Briefly rinse gel with ${\mathsf{d d H}}_{2}0$ .
40| Depurination: Soak the gel in depurination solution (sufficient volume to completely cover the gel) for $15{\mathrm{~min}}$ .   
Shake slowly in a horizontal platform.
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP Be careful not to break the gel at this point and in subsequent steps. Use a suction vacuum to aspirate the solution.
41| Denaturation: Soak the gel in denaturation solution for 30 min with gentle shaking.
42| Neutralization: Soak the gel in neutralization solution for $30~\mathrm{min}$ with gentle shaking.
43| Cut the membrane and three sheets of 3 MM Whatman paper to the size of the gel. Soak membrane and paper in $2\times$ SSC buffer.
44| DNA capillary transfer: Pour a sufficient volume of $10\times$ SSC buffer in a glass or plastic tray that is larger than the size of the gel. Place an elevated glass or plastic plate
that is larger than the gel inside the tray. Cut a sheet of 3 MM Whatman paper to completely cover the surface of the plate with all its four sides draped over the edges of the plate and immersed in the $10\times550$ buffer about half way up. Soak paper in $10\times{\mathsf{S S C}}$ buffer and carefully smooth over the plate, removing all air bubbles by pressing them away to the sides using a serological pipette. Place the gel inverted on the 3 MM Whatman paper. Carefully remove bubbles from beneath the gel. Surround the gel with plastic wrap along all four sides to ensure that capillary action takes place only through the gel. Place the membrane on top of the gel. Carefully remove bubbles from beneath the membrane. Place three layers of 3 MM Whatman paper on top of the membrane. Remove bubbles beneath each layer. Stack paper towels over the paper ${\sim}10$ cm high. Place a glass or plastic plate on top. Place a weight of ${\sim}0.5~\mathsf{k g}$ (e.g., a $500-\mathsf{m l}$ plastic bottle full of water or other liquid) on top. Leave the assembled transfer setup undisturbed for at least $12{-}16\ h$ .
45| Day 20: Remove all the paper above the membrane. Mark the position of the gel slots and the orientiation on the membrane. Remove membrane and rinse briefly in $2\times550$ . Drain excess fluid and place membrane with the DNA side facing upward on a dry sheet of Whatman paper.
