## ANTICIPATED RESULTS
We have applied this protocol to seven different human iPSC lines with similar results. We have estimated the efficiency of recovering single-copy clones, using the protocol described here, as follows. In our recent study, approximately half (51 out of 107, $47.7\%)$ of the screened clones that were obtained with the optimized transduction conditions described here (i.e., yielding transduction of less than $30\%$ of the cells $48\mathrm{~h~}$ after transduction), were found, after antibiotic selection, to contain one vector copy by ${\mathsf{q P C R}}^{15}$ . If we exclude some of these that are found, after Southern blotting, to be mixed and not clonal populations (in our data set, 2 out of 15, or $13.3\%$ ), the overall efficiency is ${\sim}40\%$ . It should also be taken into account that not all single-copy clones will express the transgene. In our study, 12 out of 13 clones expressed the $\upbeta$ -globin transgene following erythroid differentiation, whereas 8 out of 13 expressed the human phosphoglycerate kinase–driven eGFP transgene (at the undifferentiated stage) for at least several weeks after transduction in the absence of selection (longer-term expression was not assessed). These data suggest that the choice of promoter and other cis-regulatory elements driving expression of the vector-encoded transgene influence the frequency of expressor versus non-expressor clones. The specific transgene, as well as sequences of the vector backbone, is also likely to affect the probability of transgene silencing at a given genomic site. The anticipated variance in the levels of expression according to the integration site can be exploited toward the generation of more than one clone with different levels of transgene expression for experimental applications requiring ‘dosing’ of expression (Fig. 2).
Despite single-cell subcloning, in some cases, cells expanded after picking an individual colony will be found to consist of a mixture of two (or rarely more) clones with a single vector integration each. In these cases, the ${\mathsf{q P C R}}$ analysis will show a VCN of 1, but more than one band will be observed in Southern blot analysis (Fig. 6). If desired, these mixed cell populations can be separated by a second round of single-cell subcloning, using the same procedure described in this protocol (Steps 9–11 and 13–19).
The efficiency of retrieving the integration site by iPCR is highly dependent on the choice of restriction enzyme, as i­ntegration sites that are very distant from a given restriction site in the genome will not be retrieved. For this reason, the use of more than one restriction enzyme in different iPCR reactions is highly recommended. We have found that by using two different frequent cutters (HinP1I and HpyCH4IV), we were able to successfully map the integration site of our globin vector in 15 out of 18 single-copy iPSC clones.
For clinical applications using genetically modified PSCs, we have proposed five criteria for selection of safe harbor sites. These criteria include (i) distance of at least $50~\mathsf{k b}$ from the $5^{\prime}$ end of any gene, (ii) distance of at least $300~\mathsf{k b}$ from any cancer-related gene, (iii) distance of at least $300~\mathsf{k b}$ from any microRNA, (iv) location outside a transcription unit and (v) location outside ultraconserved regions of the human genome. In our survey of 5,840 integration sites of a lentiviral vector in iPSCs, we found that $65\%$ of integrations are within gene transcription units, whereas $55\%$ are within $50~\mathsf{k b}$ of transcriptional start sites and therefore close to gene promoters. Approximately $17\%$ of the sites meet the above five criteria15.
